<section id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Quantitative evaluation of the predictions of part semantics and articulation parameters</h5>

<div id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluated occupancy classification and joint parameter accuracy on the PartNet-Mobility test set, comparing with SINGAPO, as shown in Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Quantitative evaluation of the predictions of part semantics and articulation parameters ‣ 5.4. Results ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ ArtiLatent: Realistic Articulated 3D Object Generation via Structured Latents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Our method achieves competitive results.
Additionally, we computed the standard deviation (<math id="S5.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="std" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">std</annotation></semantics></math>) of predicted articulation parameters across voxels within each part and observed that the intra-part variance is generally low (see Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Quantitative evaluation of the predictions of part semantics and articulation parameters ‣ 5.4. Results ‣ 5. Experiments ‣ ArtiLatent: Realistic Articulated 3D Object Generation via Structured Latents" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, computed on the PartNet-Mobility test set and averaged over all parts). This supports the spatial consistency of voxel-wise predictions and justifies our averaging strategy.</p>
</div>
